A||||14||jlruiz67||wich type of V-8 conversion||||4||
Z||000000||jlruiz67||07-23-2000||10:56 PM||||What is the best way to go on a V8 conversion? I'm reconsidering a V8 conversion and was interested in the logitudinally mounted V8.<P>(1) the original Zumalt transverse kit,<BR>(2) the V-8 Archie transverse kit,<BR>(3) the do-it-yourself transverse V-8 kit,<BR>(4) a longitudinally mounted V-8 kit||||reg||4
Z||000001||Will||07-24-2000||12:48 PM||||(1) I don't know much about the Zumalt kit, but I hear that it hard mounts the engine.<P>(2) I think the Archie kit is the best of the kits (for SBC, obviously). The waterpump drive protruding through the wheel well is inconsequential for a number of reasons, and it uses stock axles and as many other stock components as possible.<P>(3) Doing it yourself is the most satisfying way and the best way to get EXACTLY what you want. I am doing a NorthStar conversion. This engine fits much more easily than SBC and does not screw up the weight distribution.<P>(4) Longitudinal conversions using Porscbe or similar transaxles (http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/cradle.htm) may require stretching the chassis and will almost certainly cost you your trunk.<BR>     I don't know anything about longitudinal conversions using the th425 transaxle from an Olds Toronado.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||07-24-2000||02:21 PM||||For what its worth, I went with a North Star Conversion V8 Kit. From the mid-west Fiero owners club. Im not planing to install it myself though, having some friends at a grauge do it. Im not a very good mechanic, so I figured I better not try. The kit was 2,899. And supposedly comes with everything needed to install the engine. (I havent got it yet).<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||07-24-2000||02:54 PM||||I'm thinking about doing a 4.9 caddy (Non Northstar) swap. I'd never find a cheap northstar around here and the 4.9 makes more torque and HP than my 86 Trans Am's HO 305.<BR>190 hp made that car move so 200 ought to make a Fiero scoot really well. <BR>It's also only about 10 lbs. heavier than the 2.8 I've got now.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/4_9Lengine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/4_9Lengine.html</A> <P>If you're gonna do a SBC then get the Archie kit. It is the easiest one to do of the three  store bought kits and has everything that you'd have to buy anyway if you were to do it  yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||brianp1fiero||07-24-2000||06:09 PM||||I have a Zumalt Kit and it is very good.  The engine in most of the kits is hard mounted.  But you have to remember that the engine cradle is rubber mounted to the frame anyway.  I deffinately like having the waterpump inside the engine compartment, not sticking through the wheel well.  I bought my kit in 1994 and I know for a fact Gary has been in the business since 1987.  Just make sure no matter what kit you buy do all the homework first.  Know what motor you will use what accesories you want to keep or delete and most importantly what the kit doesn't contain that you will deffinately need.  Its the little things that will nickle and dime you to death. (more like $100)||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||07-24-2000||08:40 PM||||jlruiz67,<BR> I've done the longitudinal swap with a 350 TPI and a TH325-4L auto transaxle. The 325-4L is a 4 speed version of the TH425. You will definately loose you trunk altogether, and you will need to upgrade the springs to coil-overs with stiffer springs. This combo is really to heavy for a Fiero, but it is very impressive when done. I didn't use any kit, and the only part of the job I had to farm-out was the axle adapters (uses stock fiero axles. Let me know if you'd like details and pictures of the conversion. <P>The Northstar is great, but you need to shell out $2000-$4000 just for the engine. Not me! No way! Most of my Fieros aren't even worth that much.<P>One of my current projects is a 4.9 liter Cadillac conversion like Pontiaddict is doing. It is a great fit, weighs the same as the 2.8, and has plenty of power and torque. Conversion costs are minimal, because it bolts right up to the Fiero trans. The best part is, I got the entire '91 Caddi for $350 (smashed). <P><BR>Jon<BR><P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||6
Z||000006||jlruiz67||07-24-2000||08:57 PM||||rockcrawl:<P><BR>Could you tell me if your are doing the 4.9 without a kit? what are the pros and cons on the 4.9 conversion vs the 3.4l or the 350 V8 conversion. I can't afford a nortstar so I'm not even considering it as an alternative. On the longitudinal swap with a 350 TPI and a TH325-4L auto transaxle, how much labor did you have to put on this big project and how much did it set you back $$, also in the level of difficulty how would you rate it? I'm not a pro but I'm pretty handy with tools. So many questions, so little time...<P>So far I was going to go for the 3.4L conversion but then I started looking at cost of repair and parts for the 3.4L and found that 3.4 V6 parts are not on the cheap side and that I could get parts for a 350 V8 for about half the price than the 3.4L. That is an angle that I had not tough of and haven't read a post on. For example headers are about $600 for 2.8-3.4L and I can get some block hugers for the 350 V8 for about $300. Same goes for pistons, heads, fuel injectors, etc. I find that the original cost of the V8 conversion is high but after a few years or mods, whatever comes first the 3.4 turns into a not so cheap solution for power. Now I'm debating on what V8 conversion to do. Loosing the trunk would be hard but I like to know if there is any money savings going that way vs the more traditional V8 swap.<P>Thank you everyone for your post they have been most informative. Please keep it up||||reg||14
Z||000007||rockcrawl||07-25-2000||10:38 PM||||I have had several emails requesting info on the longitudinal V8 swap. I am putting together a webpage to go into more detail than I can here. Let me just tell you, It is very labor intensive. You will need to have good fabrication skills, or be able to shell out money to someone who does. I do all my own work, and fabricate most of the parts myself, so I usually spend very little on an egine swap. <P>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||07-25-2000||11:00 PM||||Rockcrawl, for the 4.9, are you using an auto  or manual transmission. The only hangup for me actually doing the swap (I'm still considering other options) is the fact that I  want to keep my 4spd and don't want to have a manual flywheel made. Unless it still makes the swap comparable to other engine swaps in terms of power and cost. <P>How much does a 3800 V6 weigh? I'm pretty sure that it's gonna add more weight than I'd  like. I want to keep weight distribution about the same as it is now. If it's light enough, I might do that instead of the Cad motor. <P>I first thought the Archie kit was okay. But in the past couple of days, I've heard a lot of people complaining about it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||elfiero||07-26-2000||04:04 PM||||All the SBC kits on the market solid mount<BR>the engine.So I decided to manufacter my<BR>own kit so I could solid mount the cradle.<BR>I did a CNC program for the adaptor plate<BR>and designed my own mounts.I used a 440T4<BR>trans so axles were a challenge.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||07-26-2000||04:58 PM||||elfiero,<P>NOW YOUR TALKING!! I brought this topic up a few weeks ago. Seems if you solid mount the cradle and rubber mount the motor, it would greatly help with the torque steer (i.e., keep the A-arms where they are supposed to be). <P>Also, you used the expression, "I used a 440T4 trans so axles were a challenge". Does this mean that you've completed this swap? Did you move the transmission to keep the motor within the engine compartment? and by doing so, need custom axles?<P>Can you fill in some details?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||07-26-2000||07:07 PM||||Pontiaddict, I am still undecided on which trans to use. I currently have my V8 installed with a 440-T4 auto trans. If I switch over to a 5 speed, I'll need custom axles because I'm using a bigger subframe with a wider trac. A custom flywheel is available from Chris West <A HREF="http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/index.html</A>  for $220. In terms of power and cost, I think the 4.9/manual trans is a great choice. It shouldn't cost any more than a 3.4 or 3.8, and would be less than a SBC V8. Again, I think the SBC is way to heavy for a Fiero. I believe the auto trans will perform much better off the line. It eliminates driver error, and is much easier to control wheel spin. I'm pretty much set on the auto, but who wants an automatic Testarossa!?I'm not sure why the 4.9 swap is not more popular. I guess it's because of the bad rep the older 4.1 has earned itself, but personally I think the 4100HT is also a good engine, but only 125hp/180lbs! <P>My 3800 Fiero handles better than any other fiero I've ever owned or driven, but it does have a 1" rear swaybar. I'm not sure of the exact weight increase, but it is negligable. Two guys came and drove mine today, and both said they didn't notice a heavier engine, but did notice the increased power over their 2.8 Fieros.<P>I've heard mixed feeling about the Archie kit, from extreme supporters to extreme flamers. Personally I think the guys that are saying it sucks, either did not research the project, or had never turned a wrench before and thought "if it comes as a kit, how hard could it be?". Any engine swap requires a good amount of mechanical skill, and I wouldn't expect any kit to be 100% complete or correct. What Archie thinks is a good motor mount, you may not be satisfied with.<P>elfiero, mrfixit58, I think the Pontiac TransSport has the same trac width as a Fiero, and it's axles and 440T4 trans are a perfect fit in a Fiero. Of course unless the trans is offset for engine clearance. <P>A side note on Northstar, I was talking to a guy last night that said the Northstar is a great powerful engine, but it makes all of it's power at high RPM. This is true of all overhead cam engines, they are high revving engines and they are gutless at lower RPMs. I drive a Ford econoline with the 5.4 Triton OHC V8 daily. It just doesn't have the low end torque of a pushrod engine. I have never talked to anyone with a Nortstar Fiero, but I hear they are not all they're cracked up to be.<P>If anyone is interested, there are pictures of some of my conversions at my website <A HREF="http://clik.to/rockcrawl" TARGET=_blank>http://clik.to/rockcrawl</A>  but it seems to be down at the moment<BR> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000012||olympic||07-27-2000||04:13 AM||||  Northstar powered fieros do 0-60 in less than 5 seconds and the 1/4 in the 12's and all this with an indestructable tranny.  They also maintain a 50/50 weight distribution and sound like an exotic at high rpm.  I think we will see more Northstar powered Fieros as more of these engines become available from wrecked Caddys and the price comes down. I know I'm looking  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Your best...???  Losers whine about doing their best.   Winners go home and f*** the prom queen! - Sean Connery in "The Rock"||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||07-27-2000||04:10 PM||||Olympic:<BR>    A Northstar Fiero does not have 50/50 weight distribution, especially with the rather chunky 4T80E transmission. The engine is almost exactly the same weight as the 2.8, but the tranmission weighs almost 300 lbs! &lt;http://www.gmpowertrain.com/transmissions/4t80_main.htm&gt;<BR>    By comparison, I can pick up a Getrag with one hand. It can't be much more than 150 lbs.<P>RockCrawl:<BR>     If you look at the N* torque curve available at &lt;http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/northstar/46_torque.htm&gt; you will see that it has 250 ft lbs available from 2000 RPM, and ~230 available at 1000 RPM. That's not in the realm of diesels, but not what I would call "gutless" at low RPM, either.<BR>     For the record, the low output does have ~15 more ft lbs than the HO available at any RPM below 4000.||||reg||1
Z||000014||rockcrawl||07-27-2000||04:56 PM||||Hey,<BR> Uh-Oh, It looks like I got some Northstar fans excited! I was just passing along info that was given to me, so please excuse me. If I could afford one, I'd probably have one, but the fact remains that they are going for $3500 and up for a used engine. If I found one under $500 I'd buy it in a heartbeat. By the time the price of them comes down, they will have been swapped into everything, and no one will be impressed by them anymore. I'm into low budget engine swaps that work, not spending twice the value of the Fiero just to have the best power to weight ratio and most hi-tech swap in town.<P>I'm sure any 300hp engine in a fiero will be way quicker than necessary. It is so light, it doesn't matter at what RPM the engine makes the most power. But in general, I don't think a 300hp OHC engine compares to a 300hp pushrod engine.<P>My 5.4 Triton IS gutless at low RPM (in a heavily loaded van). It almost feels like someone hit the brakes when it changes gears. Its not untill its ready to shift again that it starts making power. In comparasin, the less powerfull 5.8 pushrod engine that it replaced would run circles around it. One good point about the Triton (applies to the Northstar as well), Nearly 100,000 miles on it and I haven't touched it, not even spark plugs. And they do sound cool, but I'm not paying an extra $3000 for an engine just because it sounds cool! <P>Don't get me wrong, I think the N* is an incredible engine. I guess I'm just upset that I can't afford one. Oh Well, I'll keep on looking too...<P>Later,<BR>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
